<revisionlist>
  <revision rev="1" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>e090f8162bdf0eae64e26d918174cad1</srcmd5>
    <version>0.147.0</version>
    <time>1787063751</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>New package: openai-codex 0.147.0 - OpenAI's Codex coding agent for the terminal.

Upstream: https://github.com/openai/codex (Apache-2.0), stable tag rust-v0.147.0.
Built from the codex-rs/ Cargo workspace; the npm distribution is not used
because it ships prebuilt binaries.

Naming
------
The package is openai-codex and installs /usr/bin/codex. A bare &quot;codex&quot; source
name would be ambiguous: Debian ships this project as rust-codex, OBS already
has R-codexcopd, and Arch - the only other distribution that packages it -
settled on openai-codex/codex for exactly this reason. No other RPM
distribution packages it yet, and there is no prior openSUSE attempt.

Only codex-cli is built
-----------------------
A workspace-wide build reaches the v8-poc, code-mode-runtime and code-mode-host
members, which depend on the v8 crate whose build script downloads a prebuilt
librusty_v8 archive from the network (the alternative path clones chromium) -
a hard failure in the offline build. codex-drop-v8-code-mode.patch removes
those three members from the workspace and %build asks for
&quot;-p codex-cli --bin codex&quot; explicitly. They implement the &quot;code_mode&quot; feature,
which upstream ships as Stage::UnderDevelopment with default_enabled = false,
so nothing a default user reaches is lost. Dropping them also keeps ~213 MB of
never-compiled V8 sources out of vendor.tar.zst.

Bundled bubblewrap removed
--------------------------
codex-rs/vendor/ carries a complete bubblewrap 0.11.2 C tree
(LGPL-2.0-or-later) which the bwrap crate's build script compiles and bundles.
%prep deletes it so it can be neither built nor shipped; %build additionally
exports CODEX_SKIP_BWRAP_BUILD=1 as a guard (the bwrap crate is not in
codex-cli's dependency graph today). codex-rs/linux-sandbox/src/launcher.rs
looks bwrap up in PATH first, falls back to a bundled codex-resources/bwrap we
do not ship, and panics when neither is available - hence Requires: bubblewrap
rather than Recommends.

One binary
----------
/usr/bin/codex is a multicall executable: codex-rs/arg0/ re-execs itself as
apply_patch, codex-linux-sandbox and codex-execve-wrapper from a private temp
directory and dispatches on argv[0]; everything else (exec, mcp-server,
app-server, execpolicy, sandbox, responses-api-proxy, completion, doctor) is a
subcommand. The other [[bin]] targets in the workspace exist for the npm
layout, so only codex is shipped.

License tag
-----------
Apache-2.0 is upstream's own licence; the rest covers the crates statically
linked into the binary. The set was derived offline from the vendored tree with
&quot;cargo tree --offline -p codex-cli -e normal&quot; - 853 crates on aarch64, 856 on
x86_64, every one of them declaring a licence. Electing Apache-2.0 where offered
and MIT otherwise: Apache-2.0 583, MIT 213, Unicode-3.0 24, MPL-2.0 12, ISC 10,
BSD-3-Clause 10, Zlib 6, BSD-2-Clause 2, CC0-1.0 1, CDLA-Permissive-2.0 1. Every
identifier was checked against openSUSE's accepted-licence list.

Two points worth recording, both repeated in a Legal-Review-Notice above the
tag so they do not have to be re-derived:

 * self_cell is &quot;Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-only&quot; and is the only crate anywhere in
   the graph that offers GPL. Apache-2.0 is elected, so there is no GPL
   obligation here.
 * Unicode-DFS-2016 (finl_unicode, wezterm-bidi) and WTFPL (terminfo) are
   present in vendor.tar.zst but deliberately not in the tag: they are
   reachable only through ratatui's optional termina/termwiz backends, and the
   workspace builds ratatui with default-features = false and only the
   crossterm, layout-cache and underline-color features, so they are never
   compiled.

Bundled C libraries are declared with Provides: bundled(...) - aws-lc 1.71.0
(a BoringSSL fork reached through rama-tls-rustls; there is no crate-level
switch to unbundle it), bzip2 1.0.8, libzstd 1.5.7, oniguruma 6.9.10 and
sqlite 3.51.3. These are what BuildRequires: cmake and gcc-c++ are for. The
binary does link the system libssl/libcrypto/liblzma - openssl-sys only turns
on its vendored feature for musl targets. There is no libz-sys at all;
compression comes from the pure-Rust zlib-rs, so no zlib BuildRequires.

cargo_vendor update=false
-------------------------
source_validator emits an advisory note asking for update=true. It is set to
false on purpose and the reason is recorded in _service: cargo_vendor's own
cargo-audit pass reports the upstream lockfile clean, so there is no advisory to
fix, while &quot;cargo update&quot; moves 383 of the ~1190 locked crates including 0.x to
1.0 jumps (anstream, anstyle-parse) and aws-lc-sys 0.39 to 0.44 - a wholesale
untested re-resolution rather than a security update. A future advisory should
be handled with cargo_vendor's update-crate parameter.

Other notes
-----------
 * codex-no-startup-update-check.patch flips check_for_update_on_startup to
   default off. Unpatched, the TUI queries api.github.com on every start and
   then offers an npm/brew/standalone upgrade action that cannot update an RPM
   install. Users can still opt back in via ~/.codex/config.toml. This is
   distribution policy, not an upstream bug, so no upstream PR is owed.
 * Shell completions are generated from the built binary and split into
   bash/fish/zsh subpackages with the usual Supplements.
 * There is no man page anywhere upstream or downstream, so none is invented.
   docs/ ships as %doc.
 * Build resources. Upstream's release profile uses thin LTO with
   codegen-units = 4; together with the -C debuginfo=2 the distribution adds so
   find-debuginfo.sh can split out -debuginfo, the single LTO rustc was
   measured locally at 43 GB RSS - far past what any worker offers (chromium,
   the largest package in Factory, requests 14 GB). %build therefore exports
   CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_LTO=off and CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_CODEGEN_UNITS=16,
   which costs some binary size and a little run-time performance and brings
   the package inside a 16 GB / 30 GB _constraints request. %limit_build is
   -m 4000 rather than the usual 2000 because a single rustc still wants 4-5 GB:
   at 2000 an aarch64 worker with 22 GB picked -j8 and the kernel OOM-killed
   rustc during codex-tui, while -j4 on a 16 GB x86_64 worker (3.9 GB per job)
   was fine.
 * ExclusiveArch is %{rust_tier1_arches} (x86_64 aarch64 here) - i586 is known
   to OOM rustc on a workspace this size, and the other distributions restrict
   similarly.
 * %check is an allowlist (codex-protocol, codex-apply-patch, codex-execpolicy,
   codex-file-search, codex-config) rather than --workspace. --workspace cannot
   be used at all - it reaches the members Patch0 removes - and beyond that the
   choice is build cost: compiling the test harnesses of core, tui and
   app-server would roughly double a build that already runs well over an hour,
   for tests that exercise upstream's logic rather than anything packaging can
   get wrong. The allowlisted crates need no network, container or terminal.
   The suite's genuinely environment-bound tests are #[ignore]d upstream anyway
   (a live API key for core/tests/suite/live_cli.rs, tmux for
   tui/tests/suite/resize_reflow.rs) and the landlock tests self-skip.

Build results in devel:tools
----------------------------
openSUSE_Tumbleweed x86_64 and aarch64 both succeeded, each running 645 tests
across 10 test binaries with 0 failures, and each finishing rpmlint at
&quot;0 errors, 0 warnings, 20 filtered, 0 badness&quot;. openSUSE_Slowroll, Leap 16.1
and 15.7 build as well.</comment>
    <requestid>1371460</requestid>
