  <revision rev="6" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>11c8647f0516ffcaaf7da153922aa61a</srcmd5>
    <version>0.152.0</version>
    <time>1788270707</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>openai-codex 0.152.0

Upstream: vim-mode draft search with n/N navigation, rate-limit banner
actions, credential-refresh progress (including Amazon Bedrock),
package-style MCP server names, per-tool output_token_limit, app-server
shell timeouts beyond one hour, cloud-task requests rejecting untrusted
backend URLs, and the planning tool now off by default.

Packaging notes:

- Both downstream patches apply at &quot;patch -p1 --fuzz=0&quot; with line
  offsets only, so no rebase was needed. After
  codex-drop-v8-code-mode.patch applies, codex-rs/Cargo.toml has no
  remaining v8 / code-mode-host / code-mode-runtime reference, i.e. the
  same five removals still take effect.

- The vendored crate set did not move. Compared at name+version
  granularity, Cargo.lock carries the same 1218 entries with a &quot;source&quot;
  field in 0.151.0 and 0.152.0 - zero added, zero removed, zero version
  changes. The only new lockfile entry is codex-guardian-context, which
  has no &quot;source&quot; field: it is a first-party workspace member, not a
  vendored dependency, and the first-party count is what goes 143 -&gt;
  144 here.

- &quot;cargo tree --offline -p codex-cli -e normal,no-proc-macro&quot; was re-run
  against both vendored trees: 748 third-party linked crates in each,
  with a byte-identical crate-to-licence map. License: is therefore
  unchanged and was not re-derived beyond that check. The
  Legal-Review-Notice counts moved only because first-party workspace
  members in that graph went 126 -&gt; 127 (aarch64 874 -&gt; 875, x86_64
  876 -&gt; 877, Apache-2.0 647 -&gt; 648). The parenthetical now names the
  748 third-party figure separately, so the licence-relevant number
  stops drifting every time upstream adds a workspace crate.

- cargo-audit must be run against a BARE copy of Cargo.lock: upstream
  ships codex-rs/.cargo/audit.toml with 11 ignore entries, and running
  the audit from inside codex-rs drops the count from 5 advisories to 1.
  All five are pre-existing - the crate set is byte-identical to
  0.151.0, which is already in Factory - and only one carries a CVE id:
    RUSTSEC-2026-0185 / CVE-2026-25800, quinn-proto 0.11.14, fixed
    0.11.15. NOT linked: reqwest gates dep:quinn behind its &quot;http3&quot;
    feature, which the workspace leaves off, so quinn-proto is absent
    from codex-cli's cargo tree entirely.
    RUSTSEC-2026-0194 and -0195, quick-xml 0.39.4, DoS, no CVE. Linked
    via codex-tui -&gt; syntect -&gt; plist. Fixed in 0.41.0, which is
    semver-incompatible for plist, so cargo_vendor's update-crate
    cannot reach it.
    RUSTSEC-2026-0118 and -0119, hickory-proto 0.25.2, DoS, no CVE.
    Linked via codex-network-proxy -&gt; rama-tcp -&gt; rama-dns -&gt;
    hickory-resolver. 0118 has no fixed release at all; 0119 needs
    0.26.1, semver-incompatible for hickory-resolver.
  The four linked ones need an upstream dependency bump, not anything
  this package can vendor around, so they are documented rather than
  worked around. The _service comment previously claimed the lockfile
  audited clean; it now carries the list above.

- BuildRequires: rust &gt;= 1.95 still matches upstream's
  rust-toolchain.toml pin (channel 1.95.0).

- Re-checked as still accurate for 0.152.0 and left alone: the
  Provides: bundled(...) versions (the -sys crates are unmoved,
  aws-lc-sys is still 0.39.0), _constraints, the %check crate allowlist
  (all five crates still exist), the %prep removals, and the multicall
  helper and subcommand names in %description.</comment>
